WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.000
Every edge into E is a lower bound.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.400
Put the bounds on a line: 3, 5, 7.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.200
To meet all three, x[E] must be 7 or more.

4
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.800
The smallest feasible value is the longest path.

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.000
Three blocks: A below C; A and C left of B.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.600
The minimum, 2, meets only C's bound; B lands on A.

7
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.600
The maximum, 3, meets both.

8
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.600
Could a constraint graph have a cycle?

9
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.800
Label every block with its place in pos.

10
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.400
Both 'left of' and 'below' need first in pos.

11
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.000
Along any edge the place grows: no cycle.

12
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.200
Compute all 14,400 pairs.

13
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.200
Not one overlaps.

14
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.000
But 720 do not fit 10 × 8.

15
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.267
Outline unused and density never change; they cannot compare pairs.

16
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:16.689
Swap E and D in pos: the E–D relation changes.

17
00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:21.489
Yet the coordinates are identical: both relations hold.

18
00:01:21.489 --> 00:01:26.289
Conversely, the same box can hide a different packing.

19
00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:31.978
14,400 pairs, only 9,056 packings.

20
00:01:32.378 --> 00:01:36.578
Some textbooks define 'below' as 'above'.

21
00:01:36.578 --> 00:01:42.489
It is a vertical mirror: swap pos and neg.

22
00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:46.089
Check the convention before you read on.

23
00:01:46.489 --> 00:01:52.622
Cost: n(n−1)/2 pairs, two DP passes, O(n²).

24
00:01:52.622 --> 00:01:56.422
Building the graphs and the DP do the work.

25
00:01:56.422 --> 00:02:01.022
Search uses swaps; the number of pairs explodes.

26
00:02:01.422 --> 00:02:05.689
The longest path gives the most compact coordinates.

27
00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:09.600
Take the maximum: every edge must hold.

28
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.067
Edges follow pos, so there is no cycle.

29
00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:16.467
No overlap does not mean it fits.

30
00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:20.467
One packing can have several encodings.
